451,1453,1456,1458,1460,1462,1465,1467,1469,1472,1474,1476,1478],{"class":401,"line":1431},21,[399,1433,1434],{"class":839},"    const",[399,1436,1437],{"class":413}," start",[399,1439,1440],{"class":409}," =",[399,1442,849],{"class":405},[399,1444,1091],{"class":499},[399,1446,480],{"class":405},[399,1448,1255],{"class":470},[399,1450,490],{"class":499},[399,1452,1370],{"class":409},[399,1454,1455],{"class":432},"\u002Fapp\u002Flogin\u002Foidc\u002Fstart\u002F",[399,1457,1051],{"class":409},[399,1459,1412],{"class":413},[399,1461,282],{"class":409},[399,1463,1464],{"class":413},"id",[399,1466,1135],{"class":409},[399,1468,417],{"class":409},[399,1470,1471],{"class":409}," {}",[399,1473,1281],{"class":499},[399,1475,282],{"class":409},[399,1477,1394],{"class":470},[399,1479,474],{"class":499},[399,1481,1483,1486,1488,1491,1493,1496,1498,1501,1503,1506],{"class":401,"line":1482},22,[399,1484,1485],{"class":413},"    window",[399,1487,282],{"class":409},[399,1489,1490],{"class":413},"location",[399,1492,282],{"class":409},[399,1494,1495],{"class":470},"assign",[399,1497,490],{"class":499},[399,1499,1500],{"class":413},"start",[399,1502,282],{"class":409},[399,1504,1505],{"class":413},"redirect",[399,1507,548],{"class":499},[399,1509,1511],{"class":401,"line":1510},23,[399,1512,1327],{"class":409},[243,1514,1515,1516,1519,1520,1523,1524,1527],{},"The login page offers every method the application supports and lets the user choose: the password form, and a button for each configuration ",[272,1517,1518],{},"GET \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Fapp\u002Flogin\u002Fproviders"," lists as ",[272,1521,1522],{},"[{id, name, provider}]",", whether the organization's own provider or one of the platform's shared Google and Microsoft registrations. No endpoint takes an email before the user has chosen a method, so a login page cannot be used to learn which emails have an account. An AI assistant building the page can ask the same question through the ",[272,1525,1526],{},"ApplicationService.getLoginProviders"," MCP tool.",[243,1529,1530,1531,1534,1535,1538,1539,1542,1543,1546],{},"A login belongs to the page that made it, and the API host accepts one only from a page that is one of the application's own UIs. ",[272,1532,1533],{},"GET \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Fme"," answers ",[272,1536,1537],{},"204"," while the page is signed in, and ",[272,1540,1541],{},"POST \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Flogout"," signs it out; both take ",[272,1544,1545],{},"credentials: 'include'"," as well.",[247,1548,1550],{"id":1549},"signing-in-to-a-tenant","Signing in to a tenant",[243,1552,1553,1554,1557,1558,1561,1562,1564,1565,1567,1568,1571,1572,1575,1576,1579,1580,1583,1584,1587],{},"An application whose tenants its administrators manage gives each tenant a login of its own. A page served at one of the tenant's sites, ",[272,1555,1556],{},"\u003CtenantId>--\u003CapplicationId>--\u003CuiName>"," under your organization's subdomain of the sites domain (see ",[277,1559,84],{"href":1560},"\u002Fapps\u002Fpersistence\u002Fmulti-tenancy#tenants-and-their-sites","), signs in to that tenant with the same calls as above: the API host reads the tenant from the page's host, so ",[272,1563,1367],{}," lists what the tenant's login offers — the tenant's own providers, then the application's — and ",[272,1566,1262],{}," admits only the tenant's users. A page that is not one of the tenant's sites, such as a UI running locally, names the tenant instead, with ",[272,1569,1570],{},"?tenantId="," on the ",[272,1573,1574],{},"GET"," and the start, and ",[272,1577,1578],{},"tenantId"," in the login body; the API host answers ",[272,1581,1582],{},"404"," for a tenant the application does not have, and ",[272,1585,1586],{},"403"," when a tenant's site names another tenant.",[247,1589,1591],{"id":1590},"how-oidc-works-with-your-application","How OIDC Works with Your Application",[1593,1594,1595,1617,1620,1623],"ol",{},[255,1596,1597,1598,1601,1602,1605,1606,1609,1610,1613,1614],{},"Your organization administrator creates one or more ",[272,1599,1600],{},"ApplicationOidcConfiguration","s for your application's login, or ",[272,1603,1604],{},"TenantOidcConfiguration","s for one tenant's, in the portal under the application's ",[258,1607,1608],{},"Settings → Sign-in providers"," tab, and registers the callback URL the tab shows for each, ",[272,1611,1612],{},"https:\u002F\u002F\u003CorganizationId>--\u003CapplicationId>.\u003Capp API domain>\u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Fapp\u002Flogin\u002Foidc\u002Fcallback\u002F\u003CconfigId>",", with the provider; or names one of the platform's shared Google and Microsoft registrations in the application's ",[272,1615,1616],{},"socialProviders",[255,1618,1619],{},"Your UI renders a button per provider the login offers; the button posts the configuration's id to the start at your API host, whose answer carries the URL to send the browser to",[255,1621,1622],{},"The provider returns the browser to the API host, which validates the provider's response, looks the user up in your application, or in the tenant the sign-in is to, and sets the session cookie. A shared registration returns the browser to the app server's shared host instead, which validates the response and hands the verified identity to your application's API host under a single-use code, which completes only in the browser that started the sign-in; that host looks the user up and sets the cookie",[255,1624,1625,1626,1629,1630,1633,1634,1638,1639,1642],{},"The browser returns to ",[272,1627,1628],{},"\u002F"," on the site that started the sign-in, which connects with the session; a sign-in that fails returns instead to the ",[272,1631,1632],{},"\u002Flogin"," page of your application's ",[277,1635,1637],{"href":1636},"\u002Fapps\u002Fapplication-structure\u002Fapplications-and-projects#applications","primary UI"," with an ",[272,1640,1641],{},"error"," parameter",[247,1644,1646],{"id":1645},"reusable-providers","Reusable Providers",[243,1648,1649,1650,1653,1654,1657,1658,1661,1662,1664,1665,1667,1668,1671,1672,1675,1676,1678,1679,1682],{},"Kinotic OS registers once with Google and Microsoft. The platform operator provisions these as ",[272,1651,1652],{},"ApplicationSocialOidcConfiguration"," entities, and your application offers one by naming its provider kind, ",[272,1655,1656],{},"google"," or ",[272,1659,1660],{},"azure-ad",", in its ",[272,1663,1616],{}," — no registration of your own with the provider. The registration is shared by every application that offers it, so a user who revokes it at the provider revokes it for all of them. Your organization's own providers for your application are ",[272,1666,1600],{}," entities your organization admin creates for it on the application's ",[258,1669,1670],{},"Sign-in providers"," settings tab, each registered with the provider under the application's own callback URL; the organization's own enterprise SSO for the portal is an ",[272,1673,1674],{},"OrganizationOidcConfiguration",", and an admin who wants the same provider at both copies it from one scope to the other. An ",[272,1677,1600],{}," whose ",[272,1680,1681],{},"createUserOnSignIn"," is on creates a user of your application from an identity it verifies on that identity's first sign-in, when the platform manages your application's tenants; see below.",[243,1684,1685,1686,1688,1689,1691],{},"All are kinds of ",[272,1687,274],{}," — see ",[277,1690,167],{"href":168}," for how ownership is captured by the subtype.",[239,1693,1695],{"id":1694},"user-management","User Management",[243,1697,1698,1699,1702,1703,1705,1706,1708,1709,1712,1713,1716,1717,1719,1720,1722,1723,1726,1727,1729,1730,1733],{},"Application-scope users are ",[258,1700,1701],{},"pre-created"," by an administrator before they can authenticate, unless one of your application's own ",[272,1704,1600],{},"s says otherwise. Having a valid Google account never grants access to your application by itself: the platform's shared registrations create no users. A configuration your organization registered for the application may have ",[272,1707,1681],{}," on, and then an identity that provider verifies which no user is bound to (an ",[272,1710,1711],{},"OidcBinding"," of the provider's configuration and ",[272,1714,1715],{},"sub"," to a user) becomes a user of the application on its first sign-in, with a tenant of its own, which is why it applies only while the platform manages the application's tenants. Under tenant administrators a user is never without a tenant, so there a user is created by an administrator, an invitation, or a tenant's own ",[272,1718,1604],{}," with ",[272,1721,1681],{},", which makes the identity its provider verifies a user of that tenant on its first sign-in at the tenant's login; the application's own configurations never create a user into a tenant. An identity whose verified email the application already holds under another method is refused with ",[272,1724,1725],{},"account_exists",". A configuration of your own for Google with ",[272,1728,1681],{}," admits any Google account, which is sign-up with Google for your application. Self-service signup with a form applies to the ",[277,1731,1732],{"href":163},"Organization scope"," only.",[243,1735,1736,1737,1740],{},"Your organization administrator manages your application's users from the organization's ",[258,1738,1739],{},"Members"," page:",[252,1742,1743,1749],{},[255,1744,1745,1748],{},[258,1746,1747],{},"Local users"," are created with an email and password",[255,1750,1751,1754],{},[258,1752,1753],{},"OIDC users"," are created with an email only — the OIDC subject is linked automatically on first login",[243,1756,1757,1758,1760],{},"For details on user and OIDC configuration management, see the ",[277,1759,162],{"href":163}," platform guide.",[1762,1763,1764],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .s7zQu, html code.shiki .s7zQu{--shiki-light:#39ADB5;--shiki-light-font-style:italic;--shiki-default:#89DDFF;--shiki-default-font-style:italic;--shiki-dark:#89DDFF;--